#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928} :activity/id #uuid "4c188df7-e524-54c2-8a5d-b2f16e8dfb77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130482 :tutorial/content "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1,$ then we can find the roots by solving the equation $f(x) = 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2.$\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1$ shown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "37a5be36-f2b1-5ebb-91b6-98ccbc9babaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8802 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14413a03-0cc3-58a8-9a8d-a19f98f83fa5" :step/math-academy-id 146046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128873 :question/id #uuid "c1e36290-8271-5508-9bf8-cf15a3907f75" :question/math-academy-id "e-14174"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f5682f-73b8-5c0a-b7d3-3336200843a0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb